{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16346","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16346","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16346","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juan","familyName":"Castorena","name":"Castorena, Juan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"E. Louise","familyName":"Loudermilk","name":"Loudermilk, E. Louise","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Scott","familyName":"Pokswinski","name":"Pokswinski, Scott","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rodman","familyName":"Linn","name":"Linn, Rodman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Canopy to Ground via ForestGen3D: Learning Cross-Domain Generation of 3D Forest Structure from Aerial-to-Terrestrial LiDAR"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-19T18:39:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:03:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-21T22:53:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-23T01:09:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The 3D structure of living and non-living components in ecosystems plays a critical role in determining ecological processes and feedbacks from both natural and human-driven disturbances. Anticipating the effects of wildfire, drought, disease, or atmospheric deposition depends on accurate characterization of 3D vegetation structure, yet widespread measurement remains prohibitively expensive and often infeasible. We present ForestGen3D, a cross-domain generative framework that preserves aerial LiDAR (ALS) observed 3D forest structure while inferring missing sub-canopy detail. ForestGen3D is based on conditional denoising diffusion probabilistic models trained on co-registered ALS and terrestrial LiDAR (TLS) data. The model generates realistic TLS-like point clouds that remain spatially consistent with ALS geometry, enabling landscape-scalable reconstruction of full vertical forest structure. We evaluate ForestGen3D at tree, plot, and landscape scales using real-world data from mixed conifer ecosystems, and show through qualitative and quantitative geometric and distributional analyses that it produces high-fidelity reconstructions closely matching TLS reference data in terms of 3D structural similarity and downstream biophysical metrics, including tree height, DBH, crown diameter, and crown volume. We further introduce and demonstrate the expected point containment (EPC) metric which serves as a practical proxy for generation quality in settings where TLS ground truth is unavailable. Our results demonstrate that ForestGen3D enhances the utility of ALS only environments by inferring ecologically plausible sub-canopy structure while faithfully preserving the landscape heterogeneity encoded in ALS observations, thereby providing a richer 3D representation for ecological analysis, structural fuel characterization and related remote sensing applications."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16346","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:46:17Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:46:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-23T03:04:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17514","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17514","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17514","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Tianyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengxiao","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Pengxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiwei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhi-Qin John","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zhi-Qin John","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Achilles' Heel of Mamba: Essential difficulties of the Mamba architecture demonstrated by synthetic data"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T08:38:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:19:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-21T02:41:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-22T01:15:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"State Space Models (SSMs) have emerged as promising alternatives to attention mechanisms, with the Mamba architecture demonstrating impressive performance and linear complexity for processing long sequences. However, the fundamental differences between Mamba and Transformer architectures remain incompletely understood. In this work, we use carefully designed synthetic tasks to reveal Mamba's inherent limitations. Through experiments, we identify that Mamba's nonlinear convolution introduces an asymmetry bias that significantly impairs its ability to recognize symmetrical patterns and relationships. Using composite function and inverse sequence matching tasks, we demonstrate that Mamba strongly favors compositional solutions over symmetrical ones and struggles with tasks requiring the matching of reversed sequences. We show these limitations stem not from the SSM module itself but from the nonlinear convolution preceding it, which fuses token information asymmetrically. These insights provide a new understanding of Mamba's constraints and suggest concrete architectural improvements for future sequence models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17514","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:13:48Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:13:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-22T03:17:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17444","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17444","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17444","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shohei","familyName":"Hisada","name":"Hisada, Shohei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Endo","familyName":"Sunao","name":"Sunao, Endo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Himi","familyName":"Yamato","name":"Yamato, Himi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shoko","familyName":"Wakamiya","name":"Wakamiya, Shoko","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eiji","familyName":"Aramaki","name":"Aramaki, Eiji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Filling in the Clinical Gaps in Benchmark: Case for HealthBench for the Japanese medical system"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T07:36:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:15:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-10T10:00:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:39:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-15T08:24:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-16T01:28:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This study investigates the applicability of HealthBench, a large-scale, rubric-based medical benchmark, to the Japanese context. Although robust evaluation frameworks are essential for the safe development of medical LLMs, resources in Japanese are scarce and often consist of translated multiple-choice questions. Our research addresses this issue in two ways. First, we establish a performance baseline by applying a machine-translated version of HealthBench's 5,000 scenarios to evaluate two models: a high-performing multilingual model (GPT-4.1) and a Japanese-native open-source model (LLM-jp-3.1). Secondly, we use an LLM-as-a-Judge approach to systematically classify the benchmark's scenarios and rubric criteria. This allows us to identify 'contextual gaps' where the content is misaligned with Japan's clinical guidelines, healthcare systems or cultural norms. Our findings reveal a modest performance drop in GPT-4.1 due to rubric mismatches, as well as a significant failure in the Japanese-native model, which lacked the required clinical completeness. Furthermore, our classification shows that, despite most scenarios being applicable, a significant proportion of the rubric criteria require localisation. This work underscores the limitations of direct benchmark translation and highlights the urgent need for a context-aware, localised adaptation, a \"J-HealthBench\", to ensure the reliable and safe evaluation of medical LLMs in Japan."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"draft v0.3 Code and analysis data is available at https://zenodo.org/records/17405321"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17444","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:12:08Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:12:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-16T03:10:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17209","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17209","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17209","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lourdes","familyName":"Moreno","name":"Moreno, Lourdes","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jesus M.","familyName":"Sanchez-Gomez","name":"Sanchez-Gomez, Jesus M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco Antonio","familyName":"Sanchez-Escudero","name":"Sanchez-Escudero, Marco Antonio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paloma","familyName":"Martínez","name":"Martínez, Paloma","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Prompt-Based Simplification for Plain Language using Spanish Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-21T19:28:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-13T01:31:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper describes the participation of HULAT-UC3M in CLEARS 2025 Subtask 1: Adaptation of Text to Plain Language (PL) in Spanish. We explored strategies based on models trained on Spanish texts, including a zero-shot configuration using prompt engineering and a fine-tuned version with Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA). Different strategies were evaluated on representative internal subsets of the training data, using the official task metrics, cosine similarity (SIM) and the Fernández-Huerta readability index (FH) to guide the selection of the optimal model and prompt combination. The final system was selected for its balanced and consistent performance, combining normalization steps, the RigoChat-7B-v2 model, and a dedicated PL-oriented prompt. It ranked first in semantic similarity (SIM = 0.75), however, fourth in readability (FH = 69.72). We also discuss key challenges related to training data heterogeneity and the limitations of current evaluation metrics in capturing both linguistic clarity and content preservation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"11 pages, 7 tables,"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17209","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:06:36Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:06:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-13T04:26:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16935","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16935","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16935","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lavish","familyName":"Ramchandani","name":"Ramchandani, Lavish","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gunjan","familyName":"Deotale","name":"Deotale, Gunjan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dev Kumar","familyName":"Das","name":"Das, Dev Kumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) of Vision Foundation Models for Atypical Mitotic Figure Classification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.10; I.4.9; I.5.4","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T07","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-21T05:46:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:46:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-10T07:07:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-13T01:18:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Atypical mitotic figures (AMFs) are rare abnormal cell divisions associated with tumor aggressiveness and poor prognosis. Their detection remains a significant challenge due to subtle morphological cues, class imbalance, and inter-observer variability among pathologists. The MIDOG 2025 challenge introduced a dedicated track for atypical mitosis classification, enabling systematic evaluation of deep learning methods. In this study, we investigated the use of large vision foundation models, including Virchow, Virchow2, and UNI, with Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) for parameter-efficient fine-tuning. We conducted extensive experiments with different LoRA ranks, as well as random and group-based data splits, to analyze robustness under varied conditions. Our best approach, Virchow with LoRA rank 8 and ensemble of three-fold cross-validation, achieved a balanced accuracy of 88.37% on the preliminary test set, ranking joint 9th in the challenge leaderboard. These results highlight the promise of foundation models with efficient adaptation strategies for the classification of atypical mitosis, while underscoring the need for improvements in specificity and domain generalization."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"MIDOG'25"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16935","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:00:03Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:00:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-13T04:26:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16772","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16772","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16772","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eason","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Eason","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeffrey","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jeffrey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Scarlett","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Scarlett","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyi","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Xinyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jionghao","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Jionghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paulo","familyName":"Carvalho","name":"Carvalho, Paulo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kenneth","familyName":"Koedinger","name":"Koedinger, Kenneth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AI Knows Best? The Paradox of Expertise, AI-Reliance, and Performance in Educational Tutoring Decision-Making Tasks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T18:38:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:37:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-03T09:44:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-06T01:15:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3785022.3785035","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present an empirical study of how both experienced tutors and non-tutors judge the correctness of tutor praise responses under different Artificial Intelligence (AI)-assisted interfaces, types of explanation (textual explanations vs. inline highlighting). We first fine-tuned several Large Language Models (LLMs) to produce binary correctness labels and explanations, achieving up to 88% accuracy and 0.92 F1 score with GPT-4. We then let the GPT-4 models assist 95 participants in tutoring decision-making tasks by offering different types of explanations. Our findings show that although human-AI collaboration outperforms humans alone in evaluating tutor responses, it remains less accurate than AI alone. Moreover, we find that non-tutors tend to follow the AI's advice more consistently, which boosts their overall accuracy on the task: especially when the AI is correct. In contrast, experienced tutors often override the AI's correct suggestions and thus miss out on potential gains from the AI's generally high baseline accuracy. Further analysis reveals that explanations in text reasoning will increase over-reliance and reduce underreliance, while inline highlighting does not. Moreover, neither explanation style actually has a significant effect on performance and costs participants more time to complete the task, instead of saving time. Our findings reveal a tension between expertise, explanation design, and efficiency in AI-assisted decision-making, highlighting the need for balanced approaches that foster more effective human-AI collaboration."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Paper presented at The International Conference on Learning Analytics \u0026amp; Knowledge (LAK26)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16772","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-23T02:56:14Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:56:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-06T04:09:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16989","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16989","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16989","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"He","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, He","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runming","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Runming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingyao","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Qingyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wendong","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Wendong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yupeng","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Yupeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengwu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhengwu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongxia","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Hongxia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ngai","familyName":"Wong","name":"Wong, Ngai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PTQTP: Post-Training Quantization to Trit-Planes for Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-21T09:07:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:50:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T06:14:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-29T00:32:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-01T07:10:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-05T01:11:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Post-training quantization (PTQ) of large language models (LLMs) to extremely low bit-widths remains challenging due to the fundamental trade-off between computational efficiency and representational capacity. While existing ultra-low-bit methods rely on binary approximations or quantization-aware training(QAT), they often suffer from either limited representational capacity or huge training resource overhead. We introduce PTQ to Trit-Planes (PTQTP), a structured PTQ framework that decomposes weight matrices into dual ternary {-1, 0, 1} trit-planes. This approach achieves multiplication-free additive inference by decoupling weights into discrete topology (trit-planes) and continuous magnitude (scales), effectively enabling high-fidelity sparse approximation. PTQTP provides: (1) a theoretically grounded progressive approximation algorithm ensuring global weight consistency; (2) model-agnostic deployment without architectural modifications; and (3) uniform ternary operations that eliminate mixed-precision overhead. Comprehensive experiments on LLaMA3.x and Qwen3 (0.6B-70B) demonstrate that PTQTP significantly outperforms sub-4bit PTQ methods on both language reasoning tasks and mathematical reasoning as well as coding. PTQTP rivals the 1.58-bit QAT performance while requiring only single-hour quantization compared to 10-14 GPU days for training-based methods, and the end-to-end inference speed achieves 4.63$\\times$ faster than the FP16 baseline model, establishing a new and practical solution for efficient LLM deployment in resource-constrained environments. Code will available at https://github.com/HeXiao-55/PTQTP."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Ternary Quantization, Under review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16989","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:01:23Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:01:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-05T02:43:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16582","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16582","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16582","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antonio","familyName":"Scardace","name":"Scardace, Antonio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lemuel","familyName":"Puglisi","name":"Puglisi, Lemuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francesco","familyName":"Guarnera","name":"Guarnera, Francesco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sebastiano","familyName":"Battiato","name":"Battiato, Sebastiano","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniele","familyName":"Ravì","name":"Ravì, Daniele","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Novel Metric for Detecting Memorization in Generative Models for Brain MRI Synthesis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T09:08:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:23:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-27T14:19:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-30T01:21:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Deep generative models have emerged as a transformative tool in medical imaging, offering substantial potential for synthetic data generation. However, recent empirical studies highlight a critical vulnerability: these models can memorize sensitive training data, posing significant risks of unauthorized patient information disclosure. Detecting memorization in generative models remains particularly challenging, necessitating scalable methods capable of identifying training data leakage across large sets of generated samples. In this work, we propose DeepSSIM, a novel self-supervised metric for quantifying memorization in generative models. DeepSSIM is trained to: i) project images into a learned embedding space and ii) force the cosine similarity between embeddings to match the ground-truth SSIM (Structural Similarity Index) scores computed in the image space. To capture domain-specific anatomical features, training incorporates structure-preserving augmentations, allowing DeepSSIM to estimate similarity reliably without requiring precise spatial alignment. We evaluate DeepSSIM in a case study involving synthetic brain MRI data generated by a Latent Diffusion Model (LDM) trained under memorization-prone conditions, using 2,195 MRI scans from two publicly available datasets (IXI and CoRR). Compared to state-of-the-art memorization metrics, DeepSSIM achieves superior performance, improving F1 scores by an average of +52.03% over the best existing method. Code and data of our approach are publicly available at the following link: https://github.com/brAIn-science/DeepSSIM."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to WACV 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16582","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:51:46Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:51:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-30T03:51:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16499","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16499","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16499","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lianghe","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Lianghe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meng","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Meng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huijie","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Huijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zekai","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zekai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Molei","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Molei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qing","familyName":"Qu","name":"Qu, Qing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Closer Look at Model Collapse: From a Generalization-to-Memorization Perspective"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T02:09:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:14:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-01T16:21:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:06:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-25T20:40:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-29T01:28:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The widespread use of diffusion models has led to an abundance of AI-generated data, raising concerns about model collapse -- a phenomenon in which recursive iterations of training on synthetic data lead to performance degradation. Prior work primarily characterizes this collapse via variance shrinkage or distribution shift, but these perspectives miss practical manifestations of model collapse. This paper identifies a transition from generalization to memorization during model collapse in diffusion models, where models increasingly replicate training data instead of generating novel content during iterative training on synthetic samples. This transition is directly driven by the declining entropy of the synthetic training data produced in each training cycle, which serves as a clear indicator of model degradation. Motivated by this insight, we propose an entropy-based data selection strategy to mitigate the transition from generalization to memorization and alleviate model collapse. Empirical results show that our approach significantly enhances visual quality and diversity in recursive generation, effectively preventing collapse."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2025 Spotlight paper"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16499","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:49:51Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:49:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-29T02:42:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18054","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18054","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.18054","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Indian Institute of Science, Bengaluru, India"],"givenName":"Nikhil N","familyName":"S","name":"S, Nikhil N","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["TCS Research and Innovation, Tata Consultancy Services Ltd"],"givenName":"Bilal","familyName":"Muhammed","name":"Muhammed, Bilal","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["TCS Research and Innovation, Tata Consultancy Services Ltd"],"givenName":"Soban Babu","familyName":"Beemaraj","name":"Beemaraj, Soban Babu","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["TCS Research and Innovation, Tata Consultancy Services Ltd"],"givenName":"Amol Dilip","familyName":"Joshi","name":"Joshi, Amol Dilip","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Knowledge Graph-based Retrieval-Augmented Generation Framework for Algorithm Selection in the Facility Layout Problem"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T17:29:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:49:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-16T08:38:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-17T01:30:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Selecting a solution algorithm for the Facility Layout Problem (FLP), an NP-hard optimization problem with multiobjective trade-off, is a complex task that requires deep expert knowledge. The performance of a given algorithm depends on the specific characteristics of the problem, such as the number of facilities, objectives, and constraints. This creates a need for a data-driven recommendation method to guide algorithm selection in automated design systems. This paper introduces a new recommendation method to make this expertise accessible, based on a Knowledge Graph-Based Retrieval-Augmented Generation (KG-RAG) framework. In this framework, a domain-specific knowledge graph (KG) is constructed from the literature. The method then employs a multifaceted retrieval mechanism to gather relevant evidence from this KG using three distinct approaches: precise graph-based search, flexible vector-based search, and cluster-based high-level search. The retrieved evidence is utilized by a Large Language Model (LLM) to generate algorithm recommendations based on data-driven reasoning. This KG-RAG framework is tested on a use case consisting of six problems comprising of complex multi-objective and multi-constraint FLP case. The results are compared with the Gemini 1.5 Flash chatbot. The results show that KG-RAG achieves an average reasoning score of 4.7 out of 5 compared to 3.3 for the baseline chatbot."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.18054","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:26:31Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:26:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-17T03:08:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17116","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17116","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17116","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Hang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zang","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Zang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yehui","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Yehui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengbo","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Pengbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhao","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Yuhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MCTS-EP: Empowering Embodied Planning with Online Preference Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-21T15:17:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:57:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-16T06:22:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-17T01:25:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper introduces MCTS-EP, an online learning framework that combines large language models (LLM) with Monte Carlo Tree Search (MCTS) for training embodied agents. MCTS-EP integrates three key components: MCTS-guided exploration for preference data collection, efficient multi-modal reasoning mechanism, and iterative training pipeline based on preference optimization. We theoretically prove that MCTS-EP achieves better performance bounds than conventional on-policy algorithms when the loss function is strongly convex, and demonstrate that it can be formulated as a search-enhanced variant of GAIL. MCTS-EP achieves state-of-the-art performace across serval benchmarks. In ALFWorld, it achieves 92% and 87% success rates for textual and visual tasks. In WebShop, it reaches an average reward of 0.81. MTCS-EP also reduces average interaction steps from from 18.7/19.5 to 10.2/9.9 steps in visual ALFWorld.Code available at: https://github.com/xuhang-2/Embodied-Agent-Planning"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17116","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:04:24Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:04:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-17T03:08:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17399","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17399","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17399","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pramit","familyName":"Sahoo","name":"Sahoo, Pramit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maharaj","familyName":"Brahma","name":"Brahma, Maharaj","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maunendra Sankar","familyName":"Desarkar","name":"Desarkar, Maunendra Sankar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DIWALI: Diversity and Inclusivity aWare cuLture specific Items for India: Dataset and Assessment of LLMs for Cultural Text Adaptation in Indian Context"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T06:58:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-29T16:39:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-16T07:09:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-17T01:27:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are widely used in various tasks and applications. However, despite their wide capabilities, they are shown to lack cultural alignment \\citep{ryan-etal-2024-unintended, alkhamissi-etal-2024-investigating} and produce biased generations \\cite{naous-etal-2024-beer} due to a lack of cultural knowledge and competence. Evaluation of LLMs for cultural awareness and alignment is particularly challenging due to the lack of proper evaluation metrics and unavailability of culturally grounded datasets representing the vast complexity of cultures at the regional and sub-regional levels. Existing datasets for culture specific items (CSIs) focus primarily on concepts at the regional level and may contain false positives. To address this issue, we introduce a novel CSI dataset for Indian culture, belonging to 17 cultural facets. The dataset comprises ~8k cultural concepts from 36 sub-regions. To measure the cultural competence of LLMs on a cultural text adaptation task, we evaluate the adaptations using the CSIs created, LLM as Judge, and human evaluations from diverse socio-demographic region. Furthermore, we perform quantitative analysis demonstrating selective sub-regional coverage and surface-level adaptations across all considered LLMs. Our dataset is available here: https://huggingface.co/datasets/nlip/DIWALI, project webpage https://nlip-lab.github.io/nlip/publications/diwali/, and our codebase with model outputs can be found here: https://github.com/pramitsahoo/culture-evaluation"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at EMNLP 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17399","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:11:04Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:11:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-17T03:08:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17552","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17552","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17552","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianle","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tianle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wanlong","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Wanlong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonathan","familyName":"Woo","name":"Woo, Jonathan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paridhi","familyName":"Latawa","name":"Latawa, Paridhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Deepak A.","familyName":"Subramanian","name":"Subramanian, Deepak A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alvin","familyName":"Chan","name":"Chan, Alvin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Can LLMs Reason Over Non-Text Modalities in a Training-Free Manner? A Case Study with In-Context Representation Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T09:16:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:21:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-24T05:24:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-25T00:25:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-11T09:40:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-12T01:36:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The remarkable performance of Large Language Models (LLMs) can be enhanced with test-time computation, which relies on external tools and even other deep learning models. However, existing approaches for integrating non-text modality representations into LLMs typically require additional costly supervised training, restricting on-the-fly adaptation to new domains and modalities. In this work, we explore the feasibility of integrating representations from non-text foundational models (FMs) into text-based LLMs in a training-free manner. We propose In-Context Representation Learning (ICRL) as a proof-of-concept to allow LLMs to adaptively utilize non-text modality representations with few-shot learning. Unlike traditional in-context learning, which incorporates text-label pairs, ICRL replaces text inputs with FM representations, enabling the LLM to perform multi-modal inference without fine-tuning. We evaluate ICRL on a suite of tasks in the molecular domain, investigating three core research questions: (i) how to map FM representations into LLMs in a training-free manner, (ii) what factors influence ICRL performance, and (iii) what mechanisms underlie the effectiveness of ICRL. To the best of our knowledge, ICRL is the first training-free framework for integrating non-text modality representations into text-based LLMs, presenting a promising direction for adaptable, multi-modal generalization."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17552","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:14:43Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:14:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-12T03:11:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17158","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17158","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17158","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Romain","familyName":"Froger","name":"Froger, Romain","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pierre","familyName":"Andrews","name":"Andrews, Pierre","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matteo","familyName":"Bettini","name":"Bettini, Matteo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amar","familyName":"Budhiraja","name":"Budhiraja, Amar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ricardo Silveira","familyName":"Cabral","name":"Cabral, Ricardo Silveira","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Virginie","familyName":"Do","name":"Do, Virginie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emilien","familyName":"Garreau","name":"Garreau, Emilien","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jean-Baptiste","familyName":"Gaya","name":"Gaya, Jean-Baptiste","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hugo","familyName":"Laurençon","name":"Laurençon, Hugo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maxime","familyName":"Lecanu","name":"Lecanu, Maxime","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kunal","familyName":"Malkan","name":"Malkan, Kunal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dheeraj","familyName":"Mekala","name":"Mekala, Dheeraj","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pierre","familyName":"Ménard","name":"Ménard, Pierre","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gerard Moreno-Torres","familyName":"Bertran","name":"Bertran, Gerard Moreno-Torres","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ulyana","familyName":"Piterbarg","name":"Piterbarg, Ulyana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mikhail","familyName":"Plekhanov","name":"Plekhanov, Mikhail","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mathieu","familyName":"Rita","name":"Rita, Mathieu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrey","familyName":"Rusakov","name":"Rusakov, Andrey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vladislav","familyName":"Vorotilov","name":"Vorotilov, Vladislav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengjue","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Mengjue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ian","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Ian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amine","familyName":"Benhalloum","name":"Benhalloum, Amine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Grégoire","familyName":"Mialon","name":"Mialon, Grégoire","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Scialom","name":"Scialom, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ARE: Scaling Up Agent Environments and Evaluations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-21T16:59:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:59:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-10T23:35:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-12T01:10:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce Meta Agents Research Environments (ARE), a research platform for scalable creation of environments, integration of synthetic or real applications, and execution of agentic orchestrations. ARE provides simple abstractions to build complex and diverse environments, each with their own rules, tools, content, and verifiers, helping to bridge the gap between model development and real-world deployment. We also propose Gaia2, a benchmark built in ARE and designed to measure general agent capabilities. Beyond search and execution, Gaia2 requires agents to handle ambiguities and noise, adapt to dynamic environments, collaborate with other agents, and operate under temporal constraints. Unlike prior benchmarks, Gaia2 runs asynchronously, surfacing new failure modes that are invisible in static settings. Our experiments show that no system dominates across the intelligence spectrum: stronger reasoning often comes at the cost of efficiency, and budget scaling curves plateau, highlighting the need for new architectures and adaptive compute strategies. Perhaps more importantly, ARE abstractions enable continuous extension of Gaia2 to other environments, empowering the community to rapidly create new benchmarks tailored to their domains. In AI's second half, progress increasingly depends on defining meaningful tasks and robust evaluations to drive frontier capabilities forward."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Updated authors order and acknowledgement"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17158","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:05:24Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:05:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-12T03:11:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16567","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16567","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16567","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikolaos","familyName":"Spanos","name":"Spanos, Nikolaos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maria","familyName":"Lymperaiou","name":"Lymperaiou, Maria","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giorgos","familyName":"Filandrianos","name":"Filandrianos, Giorgos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Konstantinos","familyName":"Thomas","name":"Thomas, Konstantinos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Athanasios","familyName":"Voulodimos","name":"Voulodimos, Athanasios","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giorgos","familyName":"Stamou","name":"Stamou, Giorgos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"V-CECE: Visual Counterfactual Explanations via Conceptual Edits"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T07:53:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:21:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-05T07:24:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-08T01:21:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent black-box counterfactual generation frameworks fail to take into account the semantic content of the proposed edits, while relying heavily on training to guide the generation process. We propose a novel, plug-and-play black-box counterfactual generation framework, which suggests step-by-step edits based on theoretical guarantees of optimal edits to produce human-level counterfactual explanations with zero training. Our framework utilizes a pre-trained image editing diffusion model, and operates without access to the internals of the classifier, leading to an explainable counterfactual generation process. Throughout our experimentation, we showcase the explanatory gap between human reasoning and neural model behavior by utilizing both Convolutional Neural Network (CNN), Vision Transformer (ViT) and Large Vision Language Model (LVLM) classifiers, substantiated through a comprehensive human evaluation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted in NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16567","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:51:25Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:51:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-08T02:47:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17701","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17701","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17701","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mariam","familyName":"Mahran","name":"Mahran, Mariam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Katharina","familyName":"Simbeck","name":"Simbeck, Katharina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Investigating Bias: A Multilingual Pipeline for Generating, Solving, and Evaluating Math Problems with LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T12:38:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:29:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-03T11:16:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-04T01:40:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly used for educational support, yet their response quality varies depending on the language of interaction. This paper presents an automated multilingual pipeline for generating, solving, and evaluating math problems aligned with the German K-10 curriculum. We generated 628 math exercises and translated them into English, German, and Arabic. Three commercial LLMs (GPT-4o-mini, Gemini 2.5 Flash, and Qwen-plus) were prompted to produce step-by-step solutions in each language. A held-out panel of LLM judges, including Claude 3.5 Haiku, evaluated solution quality using a comparative framework. Results show a consistent gap, with English solutions consistently rated highest, and Arabic often ranked lower. These findings highlight persistent linguistic bias and the need for more equitable multilingual AI systems in education."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published in CEUR Workshop Proceedings, Vol. 4114, edu4AI'25: 2nd Workshop on Education for Artificial Intelligence, co-located with ECAI 2025, Bologna, Italy"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17701","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:18:14Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:18:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-04T18:40:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16264","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16264","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16264","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjie","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Wenjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hange","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Hange","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingying","familyName":"Zhuang","name":"Zhuang, Yingying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xutao","familyName":"Mao","name":"Mao, Xutao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingwei","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Jingwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xudong","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Xudong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyu","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Tianyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinrui","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jinrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ParlAI Vote: A Web Platform for Analyzing Gender and Political Bias in Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-18T04:34:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:01:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T03:43:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-24T00:27:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-02T07:59:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-03T01:31:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present ParlAI Vote, an interactive web platform for exploring European Parliament debates and votes, and for testing LLMs on vote prediction and bias analysis. This web system connects debate topics, speeches, and roll-call outcomes, and includes rich demographic data such as gender, age, country, and political group. Users can browse debates, inspect linked speeches, compare real voting outcomes with predictions from frontier LLMs, and view error breakdowns by demographic group. Visualizing the EuroParlVote benchmark and its core tasks of gender classification and vote prediction, ParlAI Vote highlights systematic performance bias in state-of-the-art LLMs. It unifies data, models, and visual analytics in a single interface, lowering the barrier for reproducing findings, auditing behavior, and running counterfactual scenarios. This web platform also shows model reasoning, helping users see why errors occur and what cues the models rely on. It supports research, education, and public engagement with legislative decision-making, while making clear both the strengths and the limitations of current LLMs in political analysis."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"online demo: https://euro-parl-vote-demo.vercel.app/; Video: https://www.youtube.com/@Jinrui-sf2jg"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16264","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:44:20Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:44:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-03T02:50:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17808","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17808","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17808","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxi","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yuxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Biao","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Biao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhidong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhidong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kunqi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Kunqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenya","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Chenya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huacan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Huacan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qizhen","familyName":"Lan","name":"Lan, Qizhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ronghao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Ronghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ling","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Ling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Remote Sensing-Oriented World Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"F.2.2; I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T14:02:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:36:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-27T15:53:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-30T00:40:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-29T05:56:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-02T01:14:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"World models have shown potential in artificial intelligence by predicting and reasoning about world states beyond direct observations. However, existing approaches are predominantly evaluated in synthetic environments or constrained scene settings, limiting their validation in real-world contexts with broad spatial coverage and complex semantics. Meanwhile, remote sensing applications urgently require spatial reasoning capabilities for disaster response and urban planning. This paper bridges these gaps by introducing the first framework for world modeling in remote sensing. We formulate remote sensing world modeling as direction-conditioned spatial extrapolation, where models generate semantically consistent adjacent image tiles given a central observation and directional instruction. To enable rigorous evaluation, we develop RSWISE (Remote Sensing World-Image Spatial Evaluation), a benchmark containing 1,600 evaluation tasks across four scenarios: general, flood, urban, and rural. RSWISE combines visual fidelity assessment with instruction compliance evaluation using GPT-4o as a semantic judge, ensuring models genuinely perform spatial reasoning rather than simple replication. Afterwards, we present RemoteBAGEL, a unified multimodal model fine-tuned on remote sensing data for spatial extrapolation tasks. Extensive experiments demonstrate that RemoteBAGEL consistently outperforms state-of-the-art baselines on RSWISE."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17808","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:20:45Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:20:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-02T04:46:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17177","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17177","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17177","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowen","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Bowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Yue","name":"Yue, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fang","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Fang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hui","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"JG","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, JG","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiakang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiakang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing-Shu","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Jing-Shu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miguel Hu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Miguel Hu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Richeng","familyName":"Xuan","name":"Xuan, Richeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shibei","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Shibei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiqi","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Shiqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Teng","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Teng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tong-Shuai","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Tong-Shuai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xi","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Xi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xialin","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Xialin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaojing","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Xiaojing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xue","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Xue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuejing","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xuejing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaming","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yaming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yesheng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yesheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ying","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Ying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yonghua","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Yonghua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunduo","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yunduo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuwen","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Yuwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheqi","familyName":"He","name":"He, Zheqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyuan","familyName":"He","name":"He, Zhiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongyuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhongyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FlagEval Findings Report: A Preliminary Evaluation of Large Reasoning Models on Automatically Verifiable Textual and Visual Questions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-21T17:53:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:00:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-14T14:25:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-15T00:54:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-25T17:49:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-26T02:05:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We conduct a moderate-scale contamination-free (to some extent) evaluation of current large reasoning models (LRMs) with some preliminary findings. We also release ROME, our evaluation benchmark for vision language models intended to test reasoning from visual clues. We attach links to the benchmark, evaluation data, and other updates on this website: https://flageval-baai.github.io/LRM-Eval/"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project homepage: https://flageval-baai.github.io/LRM-Eval/ This work will also be presented at NeurIPS 2025 Workshop on Foundations of Reasoning in Language Models (FoRLM); update with trials on Gemini 3 Pro"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17177","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:05:51Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:05:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-26T03:24:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16986","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16986","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16986","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feng","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Feng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Gong","name":"Gong, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhipeng","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Zhipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingjing","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jingjing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu-Gang","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Yu-Gang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VCE: Safe Autoregressive Image Generation via Visual Contrast Exploitation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-21T09:00:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:50:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-22T06:42:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-25T01:23:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recently, autoregressive image generation models have wowed audiences with their remarkable capability in creating surprisingly realistic images. Models such as GPT-4o and LlamaGen can not only produce images that faithfully mimic renowned artistic styles like Ghibli, Van Gogh, or Picasso, but also potentially generate Not-Safe-For-Work (NSFW) content, raising significant concerns regarding copyright infringement and ethical use. Despite these concerns, methods to safeguard autoregressive text-to-image models remain underexplored. Previous concept erasure methods, primarily designed for diffusion models that operate in denoising latent space, are not directly applicable to autoregressive models that generate images token by token. To address this critical gap, we propose Visual Contrast Exploitation (VCE), a novel framework comprising: (1) an innovative contrastive image pair construction paradigm that precisely decouples unsafe concepts from their associated content semantics, and (2) a sophisticated DPO-based training approach that enhances the model's ability to identify and leverage visual contrastive features from image pairs, enabling precise concept erasure. Our comprehensive experiments across three challenging tasks-artist style erasure, explicit content erasure, and object removal-demonstrate that our method effectively secures the model, achieving state-of-the-art results while erasing unsafe concepts and maintaining the integrity of unrelated safe concepts. The code and models are available at https://github.com/Maplebb/VCE."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16986","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:01:19Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:01:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-25T04:49:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17425","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17425","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17425","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenliang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhenliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yuxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongzhao","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Hongzhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiyun","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Shiyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyuan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Mingyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujie","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yujie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyi","familyName":"He","name":"He, Xinyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenku","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Zhenku","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujia","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Yujia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evaluating Multimodal Large Language Models with Daily Composite Tasks in Home Environments"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T07:17:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:14:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-20T03:35:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-21T01:15:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A key feature differentiating artificial general intelligence (AGI) from traditional AI is that AGI can perform composite tasks that require a wide range of capabilities. Although embodied agents powered by multimodal large language models (MLLMs) offer rich perceptual and interactive capabilities, it remains largely unexplored whether they can solve composite tasks. In the current work, we designed a set of composite tasks inspired by common daily activities observed in early childhood development. Within a dynamic and simulated home environment, these tasks span three core domains: object understanding, spatial intelligence, and social activity. We evaluated 17 leading proprietary and open-source MLLMs on these tasks. The results consistently showed poor performance across all three domains, indicating a substantial gap between current capabilities and general intelligence requirements. Together, our tasks offer a preliminary framework for evaluating the general capabilities of embodied agents, marking an early but significant step toward the development of embodied MLLMs and their real-world deployment."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17425","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:11:42Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:11:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-21T03:02:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17654","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17654","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17654","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sehyun","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Sehyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hye Jun","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Hye Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiwoo","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jiwoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taemin","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Taemin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Clothing agnostic Pre-inpainting Virtual Try-ON"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T11:58:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:26:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T06:05:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-24T00:33:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-19T04:24:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-20T01:23:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the development of deep learning technology, virtual try-on technology has devel-oped important application value in the fields of e-commerce, fashion, and entertainment. The recently proposed Leffa technology has addressed the texture distortion problem of diffusion-based models, but there are limitations in that the bottom detection inaccuracy and the existing clothing silhouette persist in the synthesis results. To solve this problem, this study proposes CaP-VTON (Clothing Agnostic Pre-Inpainting Virtual Try-On). CaP-VTON integrates DressCode-based multi-category masking and Stable Diffu-sion-based skin inflation preprocessing; in particular, a generated skin module was in-troduced to solve skin restoration problems that occur when long-sleeved images are con-verted to short-sleeved or sleeveless ones, introducing a preprocessing structure that im-proves the naturalness and consistency of full-body clothing synthesis, and allowing the implementation of high-quality restoration considering human posture and color. As a result, CaP-VTON achieved 92.5%, which is 15.4% better than Leffa, in short-sleeved syn-thesis accuracy, and consistently reproduced the style and shape of the reference clothing in visual evaluation. These structures maintain model-agnostic properties and are appli-cable to various diffusion-based virtual inspection systems; they can also contribute to applications that require high-precision virtual wearing, such as e-commerce, custom styling, and avatar creation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Github : https://github.com/DevChoco/CAP-VTON"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17654","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:17:07Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:17:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-20T03:14:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18015","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18015","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.18015","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Advait","familyName":"Gosai","name":"Gosai, Advait","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arun","familyName":"Kavishwar","name":"Kavishwar, Arun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stephanie L.","familyName":"McNamara","name":"McNamara, Stephanie L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soujanya","familyName":"Samineni","name":"Samineni, Soujanya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Renato","familyName":"Umeton","name":"Umeton, Renato","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Chowdhury","name":"Chowdhury, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"William","familyName":"Lotter","name":"Lotter, William","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond Diagnosis: Evaluating Multimodal LLMs for Pathology Localization in Chest Radiographs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T16:54:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:47:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-19T01:49:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-20T01:15:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent work has shown promising performance of frontier large language models (LLMs) and their multimodal counterparts in medical quizzes and diagnostic tasks, highlighting their potential for broad clinical utility given their accessible, general-purpose nature. However, beyond diagnosis, a fundamental aspect of medical image interpretation is the ability to localize pathological findings. Evaluating localization not only has clinical and educational relevance but also provides insight into a model's spatial understanding of anatomy and disease. Here, we systematically assess two general-purpose MLLMs (GPT-4 and GPT-5) and a domain-specific model (MedGemma) in their ability to localize pathologies on chest radiographs, using a prompting pipeline that overlays a spatial grid and elicits coordinate-based predictions. Averaged across nine pathologies in the CheXlocalize dataset, GPT-5 exhibited a localization accuracy of 49.7%, followed by GPT-4 (39.1%) and MedGemma (17.7%), all lower than a task-specific CNN baseline (59.9%) and a radiologist benchmark (80.1%). Despite modest performance, error analysis revealed that GPT-5's predictions were largely in anatomically plausible regions, just not always precisely localized. GPT-4 performed well on pathologies with fixed anatomical locations, but struggled with spatially variable findings and exhibited anatomically implausible predictions more frequently. MedGemma demonstrated the lowest performance on all pathologies, but showed improvements when provided examples through few shot prompting. Our findings highlight both the promise and limitations of current MLLMs in medical imaging and underscore the importance of integrating them with task-specific tools for reliable use."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Proceedings of the 5th Machine Learning for Health (ML4H) Symposium"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.18015","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:25:37Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:25:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-20T03:14:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16848","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16848","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16848","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Atsushi","familyName":"Katsuda","name":"Katsuda, Atsushi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"An extension of the Floquet-Bloch theory to nilpotent groups and its applications"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Differential Geometry (math.DG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Mathematical Physics (math-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Dynamical Systems (math.DS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Number Theory (math.NT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Probability (math.PR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"Primary 58J50 Secondary 58J37, 58J35, 30F99","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-21T00:22:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:42:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-20T22:59:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-22T00:16:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-16T02:59:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-18T01:51:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We develop a generalized Floquet-Bloch theory for discrete torsion-free nilpotent groups by exploiting their Malcev completions. Our main result is a branching formula that relates finite-dimensional representations of a discrete nilpotent lattice to infinite-dimensional unitary representations of its simply connected nilpotent Lie group. This generalization enables to extend the following two classical asymptotic problems (i) a Chebotarev density analogue for prime closed geodesics on compact negatively curved manifolds with nilpotent covers, and (ii) long time asymptotic expansions of heat kernels on such coverings to the nilpotent setting.\n As a by-product, we derive a semi-classical expansion for the Harper operator, presenting an alternative to mathematical justification of Wilkinson's formula by Helffer-Sjöstrand.\n We conclude by proposing several avenues for future work: extensions to general hyperbolic flows and noncompact manifolds (in particular knot complements and related quasi-morphisms), connections to modified Riemann-Hilbert problems and opers. Furthermore, we give a brief comment on asymptotic behavior of knot invariants and infinite extensions in number theory."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"194 pages, 5 figures. Use of AI and LLM added, several minor content revisions, and typographical corrections"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16848","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:58:00Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:58:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-18T05:15:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18041","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.18041","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.18041","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sahil","familyName":"Shah","name":"Shah, Sahil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"S P","familyName":"Sharan","name":"Sharan, S P","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harsh","familyName":"Goel","name":"Goel, Harsh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minkyu","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Minkyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mustafa","familyName":"Munir","name":"Munir, Mustafa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manvik","familyName":"Pasula","name":"Pasula, Manvik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Radu","familyName":"Marculescu","name":"Marculescu, Radu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sandeep","familyName":"Chinchali","name":"Chinchali, Sandeep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"NeuS-QA: Grounding Long-Form Video Understanding in Temporal Logic and Neuro-Symbolic Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-22T17:15:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:48:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-14T04:05:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-17T01:18:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While vision-language models (VLMs) excel at tasks involving single images or short videos, they still struggle with Long Video Question Answering (LVQA) due to its demand for complex multi-step temporal reasoning. Vanilla approaches, which simply sample frames uniformly and feed them to a VLM along with the question, incur significant token overhead. This forces aggressive downsampling of long videos, causing models to miss fine-grained visual structure, subtle event transitions, and key temporal cues. Recent works attempt to overcome these limitations through heuristic approaches; however, they lack explicit mechanisms for encoding temporal relationships and fail to provide any formal guarantees that the sampled context actually encodes the compositional or causal logic required by the question. To address these foundational gaps, we introduce NeuS-QA, a training-free, plug-and-play neuro-symbolic pipeline for LVQA. NeuS-QA first translates a natural language question into a logic specification that models the temporal relationship between frame-level events. Next, we construct a video automaton to model the video's frame-by-frame event progression, and finally employ model checking to compare the automaton against the specification to identify all video segments that satisfy the question's logical requirements. Only these logic-verified segments are submitted to the VLM, thus improving interpretability, reducing hallucinations, and enabling compositional reasoning without modifying or fine-tuning the model. Experiments on the LongVideoBench and CinePile LVQA benchmarks show that NeuS-QA significantly improves performance by over 10%, particularly on questions involving event ordering, causality, and multi-step reasoning. We open-source our code at https://utaustin-swarmlab.github.io/NeuS-QA/."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.18041","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:26:13Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:26:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-17T03:05:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":440,"totalPages":18,"page":5},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=5\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=25\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-09-22T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-09-23T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D+AND+%28titles.title%3A%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+transformer+OR+transformers+OR+%22foundation+model%22+OR+%22foundation+models%22+OR+%22LLM+agent%22+OR+%22LLM+agents%22+OR+%22language+agent%22+OR+%22language+agents%22+OR+agentic+OR+%22vision+language%22+OR+%22vision-language%22+OR+multimodal+OR+%22multi-modal%22+OR+diffusion+OR+%22world+model%22+OR+%22world+models%22+OR+%22mixture+of+experts%22+OR+%22mixture-of-experts%22%29+OR+descriptions.description%3A%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+%22transformer+model%22+OR+%22transformer+models%22+OR+%22transformer+architecture%22+OR+%22transformer+architectures%22+OR+%22foundation+model%22+OR+%22foundation+models%22+OR+%22LLM+agent%22+OR+%22LLM+agents%22+OR+%22language+agent%22+OR+%22language+agents%22+OR+agentic+OR+%22vision+language+model%22+OR+%22vision+language+models%22+OR+%22vision-language+model%22+OR+%22vision-language+models%22+OR+%22multimodal+model%22+OR+%22multimodal+models%22+OR+%22multi-modal+model%22+OR+%22multi-modal+models%22+OR+%22multimodal+learning%22+OR+%22diffusion+model%22+OR+%22diffusion+models%22+OR+%22world+model%22+OR+%22world+models%22+OR+%22mixture+of+experts%22+OR+%22mixture-of-experts%22%29%29","next":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=6\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=25\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-09-22T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-09-23T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D+AND+%28titles.title%3A%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+transformer+OR+transformers+OR+%22foundation+model%22+OR+%22foundation+models%22+OR+%22LLM+agent%22+OR+%22LLM+agents%22+OR+%22language+agent%22+OR+%22language+agents%22+OR+agentic+OR+%22vision+language%22+OR+%22vision-language%22+OR+multimodal+OR+%22multi-modal%22+OR+diffusion+OR+%22world+model%22+OR+%22world+models%22+OR+%22mixture+of+experts%22+OR+%22mixture-of-experts%22%29+OR+descriptions.description%3A%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+%22transformer+model%22+OR+%22transformer+models%22+OR+%22transformer+architecture%22+OR+%22transformer+architectures%22+OR+%22foundation+model%22+OR+%22foundation+models%22+OR+%22LLM+agent%22+OR+%22LLM+agents%22+OR+%22language+agent%22+OR+%22language+agents%22+OR+agentic+OR+%22vision+language+model%22+OR+%22vision+language+models%22+OR+%22vision-language+model%22+OR+%22vision-language+models%22+OR+%22multimodal+model%22+OR+%22multimodal+models%22+OR+%22multi-modal+model%22+OR+%22multi-modal+models%22+OR+%22multimodal+learning%22+OR+%22diffusion+model%22+OR+%22diffusion+models%22+OR+%22world+model%22+OR+%22world+models%22+OR+%22mixture+of+experts%22+OR+%22mixture-of-experts%22%29%29"}}